{
 "description": "Test vectors for the claim a poster signs and a member's CLI checks offline (s6mt). A claim is {id, source, fields, ruleset, level, confirmed_at, checked_at}: id is the advisory id the source is looked up by and equals the fields' own id (a CVE id reads kev, a GHSA id ghsa, any other OSV id osv); fields is the reading, as spec/ruleset.json defines it; ruleset is the integer version of the ruleset that gave the level; level is critical, high, notice or none; confirmed_at and checked_at are integer Unix seconds, confirmed_at not after checked_at. The product is not in the claim: it is the product of the alert the claim is signed with. Canonical form: the JSON object with keys sorted by code point at every depth, no whitespace between tokens, strings escaped only where JSON requires it (the quote, the backslash; control characters never occur), every other character literal, UTF-8. Values are objects with ASCII keys, lists, strings in NFC with no control or format character, integers of magnitude at most 2^53 - 1, true, false and null; there is no float. Lists whose order the source does not fix have one order: packages ascending by (ecosystem, name, range, first_patched) compared by code point with null as the empty string, each entry once; cwes ascending, each once, [] when none. 'claims': each vector's 'canonical' is the exact text of the canonical form of 'claim', 'sha256' its SHA-256, and the ruleset gives 'claim.level' for 'product'. 'levels': the level the ruleset gives 'product' for 'readings', each [source, fields]. Both test suites load this file: tests/test_ruleset.py and tests/poster/test_rules.py, which builds poster-kev and poster-ghsa-page through the poster's own code.",
 "ruleset": 1,
